1
00:00:30,197 --> 00:00:34,118
ROUND 3
YOU ARE MY DESTINY

2
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:46,881
Hello, everyone.

3
00:00:46,964 --> 00:00:48,841
I am your host of The Table: K-Chef 2026

4
00:00:48,924 --> 00:00:50,301
and a K-food ambassador.

5
00:00:50,384 --> 00:00:52,720
I am Captain Cook!

6
00:01:09,737 --> 00:01:12,531
First of all,
I would like to thank all the chefs

7
00:01:12,615 --> 00:01:15,367
for making the long trip to Haeon County.

8
00:01:15,451 --> 00:01:16,869
As you know, Haeon County is

9
00:01:16,952 --> 00:01:19,622
one of Korea's key regions
with an unspoiled natural environment,

10
00:01:19,705 --> 00:01:23,334
famous for its lovely weather
and beautiful scenery.

11
00:01:23,417 --> 00:01:25,252
The water here is so clear

12
00:01:25,336 --> 00:01:29,715
that the region's home-brewed liquor
has been highly regarded nationwide

13
00:01:29,799 --> 00:01:31,550
since the Joseon era.

14
00:01:31,634 --> 00:01:35,137
The beautiful pebble beach
that stretches for a whole kilometer

15
00:01:35,221 --> 00:01:38,557
is covered in black pebbles, not sand.

16
00:01:38,641 --> 00:01:42,603
The unique sight is enough
to make any visitor gasp in awe.

17
00:01:42,686 --> 00:01:43,854
Located on the border

18
00:01:43,938 --> 00:01:46,816
between South Gyeongsang
and South Jeolla Provinces,

19
00:01:46,899 --> 00:01:48,859
Haeon County embodies a blend

20
00:01:48,943 --> 00:01:51,737
of the two regions' geographical,
climatic, and cultural key traits.

21
00:01:51,821 --> 00:01:56,325
This gave the region fertile ground
for culture and the arts to flourish.

22
00:01:56,408 --> 00:02:02,164
In fact, Haeon County is home
to many artists and artisans.

23
00:02:02,248 --> 00:02:08,003
Today's competition is actually
being held at a very special place.

24
00:02:09,672 --> 00:02:11,298
This outdoor stage is

25
00:02:11,382 --> 00:02:14,468
in an elementary school
with a long history.

26
00:02:14,552 --> 00:02:15,761
You guessed it.

27
00:02:15,845 --> 00:02:16,679
Presenting…

28
00:02:18,889 --> 00:02:20,391
Haeon Elementary School!

29
00:02:32,945 --> 00:02:34,280
This prestigious school

30
00:02:34,905 --> 00:02:38,367
has a long history
and countless distinguished alumni.

31
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:40,452
However, as more people move to cities,

32
00:02:40,578 --> 00:02:42,997
the county's population
continues to decline.

33
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:45,040
I'm sad to inform you

34
00:02:45,833 --> 00:02:49,044
the school will shut down
after the next graduation ceremony.

35
00:02:50,004 --> 00:02:53,007
Elementary schools
used to be called "national schools"

36
00:02:53,841 --> 00:02:56,844
because every Korean attended one
and still does for primary education.

37
00:02:57,428 --> 00:02:59,805
Some might not finish
middle or high school,

38
00:02:59,889 --> 00:03:02,933
but almost every Korean
finishes elementary school.

39
00:03:03,017 --> 00:03:06,145
You could say an elementary school

40
00:03:06,228 --> 00:03:08,147
is a big part of our lives.

41
00:03:09,398 --> 00:03:11,734
You could even call it destiny.

42
00:03:13,068 --> 00:03:15,779
ROUND 3
YOU ARE MY DESTINY

43
00:03:20,326 --> 00:03:22,786
"You are my destiny."

44
00:03:23,537 --> 00:03:25,414
That is the theme for this round.

45
00:03:26,123 --> 00:03:28,042
Perhaps some of you

46
00:03:28,125 --> 00:03:31,128
have already guessed the mission.

47
00:03:32,671 --> 00:03:33,505
Wait.

48
00:03:33,589 --> 00:03:37,259
Do any of you know
the upcoming mission?

49
00:03:40,012 --> 00:03:43,307
Fortunately, it looks like none of you do.

50
00:03:44,642 --> 00:03:46,268
If you catch on too early,

51
00:03:46,352 --> 00:03:50,689
it would discourage our poor crew
who worked for days on the themes.

52
00:03:52,858 --> 00:03:57,863
Without further ado,
we will reveal the mission.

53
00:03:57,947 --> 00:04:00,783
I trust this theme has kept you
in suspense the whole week.

54
00:04:00,866 --> 00:04:02,785
The mission tied to this theme is…

55
00:04:12,378 --> 00:04:13,337
Here it is!

56
00:04:13,837 --> 00:04:15,756
THE PERFECT SPREAD
TO PAIR WITH TRADITIONAL KOREAN LIQUOR

57
00:04:29,436 --> 00:04:30,437
Jib on the audience.

58
00:04:30,521 --> 00:04:32,690
Draw a big arc to the right.

59
00:04:32,773 --> 00:04:33,816
Faster.

60
00:04:51,166 --> 00:04:55,045
We have a very special guest
joining us tonight.

61
00:04:55,754 --> 00:04:57,631
She is the mastermind behind this program

62
00:04:57,715 --> 00:05:00,634
and a graduate of Haeon Elementary School.

63
00:05:01,719 --> 00:05:02,553
Everyone.

64
00:05:03,137 --> 00:05:04,430
Please welcome

65
00:05:05,139 --> 00:05:07,474
Chairwoman Moon Hae-seon
of Pyeongyeon Group!

66
00:05:07,558 --> 00:05:09,268
Give her a big round of applause!

67
00:05:19,236 --> 00:05:21,947
JANG OK-SEON
OWNER-CHEF OF ONGOJISIM

68
00:05:22,031 --> 00:05:23,824
A pairing with traditional Korean liquor?

69
00:05:23,907 --> 00:05:25,325
It's such a welcome mission.

70
00:05:26,285 --> 00:05:27,494
At last,

71
00:05:27,578 --> 00:05:31,498
we have a mission
that suits Korean cuisine, or K-food.

72
00:05:31,582 --> 00:05:32,416
DID YOU EXPECT THIS?

73
00:05:32,499 --> 00:05:33,500
Did I expect this?

74
00:05:34,418 --> 00:05:36,086
Not at all, to be honest.

75
00:05:36,170 --> 00:05:37,838
It's a difficult theme, for sure.

76
00:05:38,922 --> 00:05:42,092
But globalizing Korean cuisine
is a challenge worth pursuing.

77
00:05:42,176 --> 00:05:44,845
To globalize Korean cuisine,
we must elevate the food

78
00:05:44,928 --> 00:05:48,599
and find the perfect pairing
of dishes with Korean liquor.

79
00:05:48,682 --> 00:05:52,519
Of course, K-food is all the rage
all around the world,

80
00:05:53,062 --> 00:05:57,149
but for a nation's cuisine
to truly be enjoyed worldwide,

81
00:05:57,232 --> 00:05:59,109
it must be enhanced.

82
00:05:59,193 --> 00:06:03,781
Pairing food with alcohol
is one of the required steps.

83
00:06:03,864 --> 00:06:06,909
Alcohol is about more
than just getting drunk.

84
00:06:06,992 --> 00:06:08,494
CHA WOO-JIN
EXECUTIVE CHEF OF EXCEPTION

85
00:06:08,577 --> 00:06:10,537
A drink served with a meal

86
00:06:10,621 --> 00:06:14,500
can enhance a dish's strengths
and compensate for its weaknesses.

87
00:06:15,667 --> 00:06:18,253
The French drink wine,
and the Japanese drink sake.

88
00:06:18,337 --> 00:06:20,881
I'd say each country's
alcoholic beverage and cuisine

89
00:06:20,964 --> 00:06:22,883
complemented each other.

90
00:06:23,467 --> 00:06:25,385
Pairing food with alcohol.

91
00:06:25,469 --> 00:06:29,014
It wouldn't be a stretch
to say that accompaniment

92
00:06:29,098 --> 00:06:32,101
helped improve recipes,
alcohol, and even culture.

93
00:06:32,184 --> 00:06:34,978
For that reason, we came up with missions

94
00:06:35,813 --> 00:06:38,899
the likes of which
you've never seen before.

95
00:06:41,527 --> 00:06:42,778
Prepare the perfect spread

96
00:06:43,612 --> 00:06:47,616
to pair with Korean traditional liquor,
a spread of destiny, if you will.

97
00:06:47,699 --> 00:06:50,661
PARK JE-HYEON
OWNER-CHEF OF BINGSAK

98
00:06:50,744 --> 00:06:51,662
Traditional liquor, huh?

99
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:54,998
Korean traditional liquor.

100
00:06:55,082 --> 00:06:56,333
CHRIS K.
HEAD CHEF OF FAMIGLIA

101
00:06:56,416 --> 00:06:58,210
I don't know much
about Korean traditional liquor.

102
00:06:59,169 --> 00:07:02,798
I've used Korean black raspberry wine
in my cooking a few times.

103
00:07:02,881 --> 00:07:05,467
Additionally, give the street food
you enjoyed as teenagers

104
00:07:05,551 --> 00:07:08,554
a luxurious makeover
to go with traditional liquor.

105
00:07:08,637 --> 00:07:10,764
YANG JA-RYONG
HEAD CHEF OF GOLDEN DRAGON

106
00:07:10,848 --> 00:07:13,058
Street food?

107
00:07:13,142 --> 00:07:15,727
Things like sundae and tteokbokki, right?

108
00:07:16,728 --> 00:07:20,566
Had I known, I would've gone
to more Korean snack bars back in the day.

109
00:07:20,649 --> 00:07:22,067
ISABEL DOMINGUEZ
HEAD CHEF OF INTEGRAL

110
00:07:22,151 --> 00:07:23,443
I'm just grateful.

111
00:07:24,570 --> 00:07:26,321
I was upset I got eliminated so early.

112
00:07:26,405 --> 00:07:29,324
I'd like to thank

113
00:07:29,408 --> 00:07:32,786
the production crew and other chefs
with all my heart

114
00:07:32,870 --> 00:07:34,079
for allowing me to return.

115
00:07:34,163 --> 00:07:36,999
Of course, I'm most grateful to Joshua

116
00:07:37,833 --> 00:07:39,418
for inviting me to join his team.

117
00:07:39,501 --> 00:07:41,378
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026
ROUND 3: YOU ARE MY DESTINY

118
00:07:42,087 --> 00:07:44,256
In this round,
you have two missions to complete.

119
00:07:44,339 --> 00:07:46,300
Create two dishes that pair well

120
00:07:46,383 --> 00:07:48,468
with traditional liquor
produced in this region.

121
00:07:48,552 --> 00:07:50,929
In other words, prepare
the perfect spread to go with the drink.

122
00:07:51,013 --> 00:07:54,474
The dishes must be based
on street food from your memories.

123
00:07:54,558 --> 00:07:56,393
That is to say, elevate Korean snack food.

124
00:08:14,369 --> 00:08:17,414
So this is Pyeongyeon's training center?

125
00:08:17,915 --> 00:08:19,708
It's in the middle of nowhere.

126
00:08:20,792 --> 00:08:22,044
What a spot.

127
00:08:22,586 --> 00:08:23,921
It looks like a five-star hotel.

128
00:08:24,671 --> 00:08:26,757
Guess they maintain it to high standards.

129
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:27,966
Looks like it.

130
00:08:40,062 --> 00:08:42,689
They did say they gave us a suite.
It's pretty nice.

131
00:08:43,815 --> 00:08:45,692
There's another living room that way.

132
00:08:45,776 --> 00:08:47,778
And we have our own bathrooms.

133
00:08:47,861 --> 00:08:50,197
A whole kitchen too. I'm impressed.

134
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:52,574
The rooms seem to have
the same size and layout.

135
00:08:53,242 --> 00:08:55,869
We'll take the living room
and bedrooms on that side.

136
00:08:58,538 --> 00:08:59,623
Shall we check it out?

137
00:08:59,706 --> 00:09:00,666
Yes, Chef.

138
00:09:42,624 --> 00:09:43,542
A lucky charm?

139
00:09:44,584 --> 00:09:45,419
Well…

140
00:09:46,503 --> 00:09:47,379
It's not that.

141
00:09:49,506 --> 00:09:53,051
My birth mom wrapped this scarf
around my neck before we parted.

142
00:09:54,594 --> 00:09:57,264
My American adoptive parents

143
00:09:58,265 --> 00:10:00,017
kept it safe all this while.

144
00:10:02,436 --> 00:10:03,854
I don't put that much weight on it.

145
00:10:04,896 --> 00:10:05,731
But I do feel

146
00:10:06,606 --> 00:10:07,733
more at ease…

147
00:10:09,735 --> 00:10:11,028
when I have it out.

148
00:10:18,035 --> 00:10:18,952
Talk to me.

149
00:10:19,453 --> 00:10:20,996
What on earth are you thinking?

150
00:10:22,539 --> 00:10:23,957
You don't know why I'm asking?

151
00:10:24,041 --> 00:10:25,334
Why did you call me?

152
00:10:25,417 --> 00:10:28,170
You should've asked for Chef Park.
He has more experience.

153
00:10:29,254 --> 00:10:31,631
Are you even trying to win?

154
00:10:33,925 --> 00:10:34,760
I'd like to hear

155
00:10:35,677 --> 00:10:36,678
the answer too.

156
00:10:38,138 --> 00:10:40,265
You called up a novice
who doesn't even know the basics.

157
00:10:42,684 --> 00:10:43,852
What's the plan?

158
00:10:50,609 --> 00:10:53,028
To be honest, I have no clue what to do.

159
00:10:53,111 --> 00:10:56,531
As you know, I'm not a chef.

160
00:10:56,615 --> 00:10:58,492
I don't have the skills,
and I know almost nothing.

161
00:10:58,575 --> 00:11:01,244
I'm a total nuisance. That's what I am.

162
00:11:02,954 --> 00:11:06,792
Why on earth did Chris ask me to join him?

163
00:11:07,667 --> 00:11:11,838
They said they'd reveal what liquor to use
on the day of the shoot.

164
00:11:12,672 --> 00:11:15,967
That means the selection process is key.

165
00:11:17,803 --> 00:11:18,762
I agree.

166
00:11:20,055 --> 00:11:21,723
The hint we got in our van

167
00:11:22,682 --> 00:11:24,684
likely refers to the traditional liquor.

168
00:11:44,329 --> 00:11:47,958
I even wondered
if he's trying to mess with me.

169
00:11:48,041 --> 00:11:50,210
But why would he want to mess with me?

170
00:11:50,293 --> 00:11:51,461
It's either that

171
00:11:52,295 --> 00:11:53,839
or he's sick of competing.

172
00:11:53,922 --> 00:11:55,924
Maybe he wants to go back to the US.

173
00:11:57,384 --> 00:11:58,927
If that's not it either…

174
00:12:02,222 --> 00:12:03,265
Is he insane?

175
00:12:05,350 --> 00:12:08,311
HINT: CANNOT ADDRESS
HIS FATHER AS "FATHER"

176
00:12:08,395 --> 00:12:10,272
CANNOT ADDRESS HIS FATHER AS "FATHER"

177
00:12:15,652 --> 00:12:18,238
Does anything come to mind?

178
00:12:18,321 --> 00:12:19,239
Not at all.

179
00:12:22,242 --> 00:12:23,410
That phrase.

180
00:12:23,493 --> 00:12:25,412
Isn't it from The Tale of Hong Gil-dong?

181
00:12:26,872 --> 00:12:27,873
It is.

182
00:12:27,956 --> 00:12:29,749
Hong Gil-dong was an illegitimate son

183
00:12:30,542 --> 00:12:33,962
and had to address his father
with his official title.

184
00:12:34,463 --> 00:12:38,008
Does the liquor
have something to do with that tale?

185
00:12:39,050 --> 00:12:39,926
Could it?

186
00:12:40,510 --> 00:12:43,513
Should I look up
what wines are mentioned in it?

187
00:12:43,597 --> 00:12:44,806
No.

188
00:12:44,890 --> 00:12:47,267
They wouldn't have given us
such a one-dimensional hint.

189
00:12:47,350 --> 00:12:48,393
The production crew

190
00:12:50,020 --> 00:12:51,521
isn't that simple-minded.

191
00:12:51,605 --> 00:12:54,483
It's really hard to pair anything
with Korean traditional liquor.

192
00:12:55,984 --> 00:12:58,111
Makgeolli goes down smoothly,

193
00:12:58,195 --> 00:13:00,614
but it's fizzy and lacks body.

194
00:13:00,697 --> 00:13:02,324
It goes better with casual dining.

195
00:13:03,742 --> 00:13:06,661
Wines made with medicinal herbs
have too strong an aroma.

196
00:13:06,745 --> 00:13:08,246
It almost overpowers the food.

197
00:13:09,331 --> 00:13:12,125
Traditional liquors are mostly yakju,
rice wine made with medicinal herbs.

198
00:13:13,043 --> 00:13:15,504
Should we go with a hard liquor then?

199
00:13:24,888 --> 00:13:25,931
What do you think this means?

200
00:13:27,641 --> 00:13:30,435
Sorry. I have no idea at all, Chef.

201
00:13:33,063 --> 00:13:34,981
Oh man…

202
00:13:35,065 --> 00:13:37,275
I only know really strong Chinese liquors.

203
00:13:37,359 --> 00:13:39,402
I'm of no help at all.

204
00:13:41,321 --> 00:13:43,657
This is so frustrating.

205
00:13:44,741 --> 00:13:45,575
Don't you think

206
00:13:46,701 --> 00:13:49,704
Japanese drinks are closer to ours?

207
00:13:50,205 --> 00:13:52,290
-They have their own soju too.
-Right.

208
00:13:53,625 --> 00:13:54,459
Park-san.

209
00:13:55,126 --> 00:13:59,089
You're Japanese.
You must know all about Japanese drinks.

210
00:14:03,176 --> 00:14:05,804
When it comes to this mission,

211
00:14:05,887 --> 00:14:08,557
you have a much greater advantage than--

212
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:10,058
I'm not Japanese.

213
00:14:14,563 --> 00:14:17,983
My great-grandfather was taken to Japan
during the Japanese colonial period.

214
00:14:19,109 --> 00:14:22,654
My grandfather was born in Japan,
and so were my father and I.

215
00:14:22,737 --> 00:14:25,198
I'm fourth-generation Korean-Japanese.

216
00:14:27,867 --> 00:14:29,911
My grandmother is Japanese.

217
00:14:31,496 --> 00:14:32,998
I'm of Korean

218
00:14:33,873 --> 00:14:35,667
and Japanese descent.

219
00:14:36,376 --> 00:14:38,962
In Japan,
I was teased for being half-and-half.

220
00:14:40,839 --> 00:14:42,924
I'm a naturalized Korean now,

221
00:14:44,342 --> 00:14:46,553
but people still think I'm half-and-half.

222
00:14:46,636 --> 00:14:47,596
But…

223
00:14:49,806 --> 00:14:51,558
my roots are Korean.

224
00:14:51,641 --> 00:14:54,311
My father always told me

225
00:14:55,145 --> 00:14:56,062
I should never forget

226
00:14:57,314 --> 00:14:59,441
my Korean roots.

227
00:14:59,524 --> 00:15:01,443
How about jeongjong?

228
00:15:03,695 --> 00:15:07,449
I remember seeing my mom offer it
during a memorial rite.

229
00:15:07,532 --> 00:15:10,035
It has a light and refreshing flavor.

230
00:15:10,118 --> 00:15:12,996
It would go well with any kind of food.

231
00:15:14,539 --> 00:15:16,750
I think it's a good traditional liquor
for this mission.

232
00:15:17,626 --> 00:15:18,918
You want to use Masamune?

233
00:15:19,836 --> 00:15:20,962
What?

234
00:15:22,964 --> 00:15:23,965
You two.

235
00:15:26,509 --> 00:15:27,761
Do you even know Korean cuisine?

236
00:15:29,679 --> 00:15:32,474
I didn't know that jeongjong was a sake

237
00:15:32,557 --> 00:15:34,726
that was popularized
under the Japanese colonial period.

238
00:15:36,019 --> 00:15:37,771
I was told it was used for memorial rites,

239
00:15:39,439 --> 00:15:41,107
so I assumed it was traditionally Korean.

240
00:15:41,941 --> 00:15:43,276
Our most important task

241
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:45,445
is to gain info on this region.

242
00:15:46,696 --> 00:15:49,866
What the specialties are,
what ingredients are well-known

243
00:15:49,949 --> 00:15:51,534
and what the locals like to eat.

244
00:15:51,618 --> 00:15:53,828
I think we should look around the markets.

245
00:15:53,912 --> 00:15:56,539
We're by the sea,
so we should visit the fish markets too.

246
00:15:56,623 --> 00:15:58,625
We must also tour the snack bars
close to the schools.

247
00:15:59,709 --> 00:16:00,752
Remember this.

248
00:16:01,294 --> 00:16:03,338
If any of us make even one small mistake,

249
00:16:04,005 --> 00:16:05,256
both teams get eliminated.

250
00:16:10,512 --> 00:16:11,596
Don't worry.

251
00:16:12,931 --> 00:16:14,391
I won't lose.

252
00:16:17,143 --> 00:16:18,353
A live broadcast?

253
00:16:18,436 --> 00:16:20,605
Yes. A live broadcast.

254
00:16:21,606 --> 00:16:26,319
You want us to go live on Sunday
instead of taping the show?

255
00:16:27,612 --> 00:16:29,239
It's not that complicated.

256
00:16:29,989 --> 00:16:33,410
Everything's set up.
We just go live instead of taping.

257
00:16:33,493 --> 00:16:34,744
The problem with that is…

258
00:16:35,328 --> 00:16:37,706
we have less than four days to prepare.

259
00:16:37,789 --> 00:16:40,542
Don't you think
there's too little time, ma'am?

260
00:16:41,751 --> 00:16:44,087
I'll send over a backup team
and a broadcasting team.

261
00:16:48,508 --> 00:16:49,676
What's the matter?

262
00:16:49,759 --> 00:16:51,469
Isn't this an awesome idea?

263
00:16:51,553 --> 00:16:54,431
Ratings, popularity.
Everything's on the up.

264
00:16:54,514 --> 00:16:57,851
We can boost all the numbers
with one live show!

265
00:16:57,934 --> 00:17:00,687
We'll air the taped episode as planned

266
00:17:00,770 --> 00:17:03,064
then go live with the next installment.

267
00:17:03,148 --> 00:17:05,024
"You Are My Destiny," part two!

268
00:17:05,108 --> 00:17:07,694
I guarantee you that the ratings

269
00:17:08,361 --> 00:17:09,487
will double.

270
00:17:10,113 --> 00:17:11,698
Double!

271
00:17:11,781 --> 00:17:13,825
Double!

272
00:17:14,659 --> 00:17:16,995
Can we adjust
the broadcasting schedule in time?

273
00:17:17,078 --> 00:17:18,955
We'll have to drop a few other programs.

274
00:17:19,038 --> 00:17:22,000
-Companies advertising on those shows--
-Listen up, Ms. Seon.

275
00:17:23,501 --> 00:17:28,256
Who would dare complain
about my executive decision?

276
00:17:30,925 --> 00:17:31,760
Huh?

277
00:17:32,719 --> 00:17:34,637
That's where it began, apparently.

278
00:17:34,721 --> 00:17:37,515
In the Goryeo era,
they produced distilled liquor.

279
00:17:37,599 --> 00:17:40,143
Then came the Joseon era.

280
00:17:40,852 --> 00:17:42,604
The golden age of liquor

281
00:17:42,687 --> 00:17:44,731
-was during the Joseon era…
-Chef Park was up to a lot.

282
00:17:46,357 --> 00:17:47,275
Yeah.

283
00:17:47,358 --> 00:17:50,028
I knew he had
a traditional liquor sommelier license

284
00:17:50,111 --> 00:17:51,905
but to have a whole channel on the topic…

285
00:17:55,366 --> 00:17:57,952
This is exactly why
you should've picked him.

286
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:07,378
They were called home-brewed liquor.

287
00:18:07,462 --> 00:18:10,924
Each was made with different techniques,
and they tasted different too.

288
00:18:11,007 --> 00:18:13,593
Wine wasn't just wine.

289
00:18:13,676 --> 00:18:16,304
It was a tradition and a technique
passed down in the family.

290
00:18:17,013 --> 00:18:19,974
There were many different kinds. Soju…

291
00:18:25,855 --> 00:18:27,524
To be honest,

292
00:18:27,607 --> 00:18:30,777
other than soju and makgeolli,

293
00:18:30,860 --> 00:18:34,030
I haven't tried
any traditional Korean alcohol.

294
00:18:34,113 --> 00:18:37,075
But those videos got me hooked.

295
00:18:37,158 --> 00:18:40,036
Now I want to know
what traditional liquor tastes like.

296
00:18:40,119 --> 00:18:40,995
Right.

297
00:18:41,079 --> 00:18:44,415
Traditional Korean drinks could go
surprisingly well with various dishes.

298
00:18:44,499 --> 00:18:47,085
Appetizers, mains, desserts, everything.

299
00:18:47,168 --> 00:18:48,002
I agree.

300
00:18:48,837 --> 00:18:50,129
It's a lovely day.

301
00:18:50,213 --> 00:18:52,340
Why don't we buy some drinks and snacks

302
00:18:52,423 --> 00:18:54,717
and taste-test them on the beach?
How's that?

303
00:18:55,301 --> 00:18:57,053
-I'd love that.
-Jeez.

304
00:18:58,805 --> 00:18:59,722
Look here.

305
00:19:00,265 --> 00:19:01,474
Are you on a trip?

306
00:19:02,809 --> 00:19:03,768
Are you here on holiday?

307
00:19:05,854 --> 00:19:07,021
I just meant…

308
00:19:07,105 --> 00:19:09,983
If you have time to kill,
work on your knife skills.

309
00:19:11,568 --> 00:19:13,152
Don't drag us down during the competition.

310
00:19:13,236 --> 00:19:15,280
Do you even think at all?

311
00:19:15,363 --> 00:19:16,698
That was a bit too harsh.

312
00:19:19,909 --> 00:19:22,245
She's a teammate you must work with.

313
00:19:30,795 --> 00:19:31,754
Tell me what you've got.

314
00:19:34,007 --> 00:19:34,841
What?

315
00:19:34,924 --> 00:19:36,009
Have you poached something?

316
00:19:36,718 --> 00:19:38,761
What about confit?
No way you'd know sous vide.

317
00:19:38,845 --> 00:19:41,180
Have you made pasta from scratch before?

318
00:19:41,264 --> 00:19:42,891
How many types of pasta can you name?

319
00:19:43,766 --> 00:19:47,020
Do you know what agar,
xanthan gum, or maltodextrin is?

320
00:19:54,068 --> 00:19:56,946
Do you know what I really hate doing?

321
00:20:01,159 --> 00:20:03,077
It's cleaning up after amateurs.

322
00:20:04,495 --> 00:20:05,830
Amateurs who don't know

323
00:20:07,290 --> 00:20:08,291
what they're doing.

324
00:20:13,296 --> 00:20:14,923
I expect you to clean up

325
00:20:16,466 --> 00:20:17,550
your own mess.

326
00:20:18,843 --> 00:20:19,719
Chris K.

327
00:20:20,595 --> 00:20:23,097
If your team so much as
makes a mistake in this round

328
00:20:23,181 --> 00:20:25,058
or if we get eliminated
because of you two…

329
00:20:27,810 --> 00:20:29,812
I won't let you off lightly.

330
00:20:42,617 --> 00:20:43,952
You're laughing?

331
00:20:51,542 --> 00:20:53,127
"You should

332
00:20:53,711 --> 00:20:56,965
mind your own business."

333
00:21:01,010 --> 00:21:03,930
I watched this film a few days ago,
and that line came up.

334
00:21:04,514 --> 00:21:05,807
Pretty amusing, right?

335
00:21:08,184 --> 00:21:09,978
What did you just say?

336
00:21:10,937 --> 00:21:12,146
Keep up the good work then.

337
00:21:20,238 --> 00:21:22,198
That damn punk keeps talking casually.

338
00:21:24,450 --> 00:21:25,743
He's a year younger than me.

339
00:21:27,620 --> 00:21:30,039
The main ingredient is mixed grains.

340
00:21:30,123 --> 00:21:32,583
What makes this liquor special

341
00:21:32,667 --> 00:21:36,421
is the fact that it's distilled
after the fermentation.

342
00:21:36,504 --> 00:21:39,549
As a result, the drink is clear
and relatively stronger.

343
00:21:46,681 --> 00:21:47,515
How is it?

344
00:21:49,726 --> 00:21:52,520
I like the faint but unique aroma.

345
00:21:52,603 --> 00:21:53,604
-Right?
-My gosh.

346
00:21:58,568 --> 00:22:01,487
"Ryu. Smoky."

347
00:22:03,322 --> 00:22:04,157
Have a taste.

348
00:22:44,781 --> 00:22:48,659
The tteokbokki sauce
masks the meaty smell effectively.

349
00:22:48,743 --> 00:22:52,413
That explains why kids
love to dip the sundae in the sauce.

350
00:22:57,585 --> 00:22:58,920
I looked it up online.

351
00:22:59,003 --> 00:23:01,380
Gwangju's preferred sauce is
vinegared gochujang sauce,

352
00:23:02,131 --> 00:23:04,300
Busan's is makjang,

353
00:23:05,009 --> 00:23:06,010
and Jeju's is soy sauce.

354
00:23:06,094 --> 00:23:07,053
Isn't that fun?

355
00:23:30,868 --> 00:23:32,078
How is it?

356
00:23:39,836 --> 00:23:40,962
It's as we thought.

357
00:23:42,004 --> 00:23:45,508
The spicy gochujang and sweet makgeolli
don't clash too much.

358
00:23:46,217 --> 00:23:49,137
You're right.
They complement each other rather well.

359
00:23:49,220 --> 00:23:50,263
Really?

360
00:23:58,813 --> 00:23:59,981
You're right.

361
00:24:01,399 --> 00:24:02,859
These foods won't go well

362
00:24:02,942 --> 00:24:05,444
with a fancy, dry liquor
that isn't makgeolli.

363
00:24:07,780 --> 00:24:10,116
What if we find a way
to neutralize the gochujang?

364
00:24:11,033 --> 00:24:13,202
Our task is to reinvent
a street food people love.

365
00:24:13,286 --> 00:24:15,037
We can't skip tteokbokki.

366
00:24:15,121 --> 00:24:18,624
It's fine if we're told to cook something
to pair with makgeolli.

367
00:24:20,877 --> 00:24:21,711
If not…

368
00:24:27,967 --> 00:24:28,801
Let's go outside.

369
00:24:28,885 --> 00:24:31,095
We could do with a change of scenery.

370
00:24:40,730 --> 00:24:41,647
Chris?

371
00:24:51,407 --> 00:24:52,408
It's beautiful.

372
00:24:53,534 --> 00:24:54,410
It is.

373
00:24:56,120 --> 00:24:58,831
There are many beautiful beaches
in South Carolina too,

374
00:24:59,457 --> 00:25:01,250
but not a pebble beach like this.

375
00:25:02,877 --> 00:25:04,045
It's a small beach,

376
00:25:04,754 --> 00:25:05,755
but it's quite unique.

377
00:25:06,547 --> 00:25:08,049
I've never been to a place like this.

378
00:25:08,674 --> 00:25:09,675
I like it.

379
00:25:16,807 --> 00:25:18,434
The pebbles are so pretty.

380
00:25:19,143 --> 00:25:21,187
They're rounded with no sharp edges.

381
00:25:22,605 --> 00:25:24,607
We could use them as decorations.

382
00:25:55,304 --> 00:25:56,180
Chris.

383
00:25:57,515 --> 00:25:58,724
Yes?

384
00:26:00,101 --> 00:26:02,895
I heard you left Korea
as a child, like me.

385
00:26:03,771 --> 00:26:06,524
What mental image
do you have of the country?

386
00:26:20,830 --> 00:26:23,666
Sorry. That was too vague, wasn't it?

387
00:26:25,876 --> 00:26:30,256
Your food from the first two rounds
shocked me a great deal.

388
00:26:31,090 --> 00:26:32,758
Not only were they creative,

389
00:26:34,844 --> 00:26:35,928
but they tasted incredible.

390
00:26:37,263 --> 00:26:40,141
I wondered,
"What kind of experience and training

391
00:26:40,224 --> 00:26:41,809
would allow you to create such dishes?"

392
00:26:51,360 --> 00:26:52,611
What about you?

393
00:26:55,156 --> 00:26:56,449
What image do you have

394
00:26:57,908 --> 00:26:59,201
of Korea?

395
00:27:06,417 --> 00:27:07,376
Pink.

396
00:27:08,502 --> 00:27:09,545
Pink?

397
00:27:19,930 --> 00:27:21,891
I'll be back soon.

398
00:27:23,225 --> 00:27:25,019
Stay right here and wait for me.

399
00:27:25,102 --> 00:27:26,520
Okay, Yeong-ho?

400
00:27:46,624 --> 00:27:47,458
Mom.

401
00:27:48,292 --> 00:27:49,126
Yes?

402
00:27:49,877 --> 00:27:52,671
Will we have jjajangmyeon
when you get back?

403
00:27:54,382 --> 00:27:55,299
Jjajangmyeon?

404
00:27:55,383 --> 00:27:56,384
Yeah.

405
00:27:56,467 --> 00:27:58,511
I want jjajangmyeon.

406
00:28:00,054 --> 00:28:02,515
And sweet and sour pork too.

407
00:28:04,392 --> 00:28:06,185
Okay. Sure.

408
00:28:06,268 --> 00:28:08,187
I'm so excited.

409
00:28:10,272 --> 00:28:11,565
When I come back,

410
00:28:13,359 --> 00:28:14,318
we can go

411
00:28:15,361 --> 00:28:17,029
have jjajangmyeon together.

412
00:28:18,239 --> 00:28:19,281
And sweet and sour pork.

413
00:28:34,046 --> 00:28:37,508
My mother didn't come back for me.

414
00:28:38,008 --> 00:28:39,927
When the closing time approached,

415
00:28:40,010 --> 00:28:42,596
the manager took me
to a nearby police station.

416
00:28:45,266 --> 00:28:46,851
They found a note in my pocket

417
00:28:46,934 --> 00:28:50,062
asking them to take good care of me.

418
00:28:52,523 --> 00:28:55,609
They couldn't find my mother after that.

419
00:28:56,110 --> 00:28:58,654
Parents abandoned their kids
a lot in those days.

420
00:29:02,741 --> 00:29:04,326
I grew up wondering

421
00:29:06,162 --> 00:29:07,788
if she didn't come back

422
00:29:07,872 --> 00:29:10,833
because I wanted jjajangmyeon
and sweet and sour pork.

423
00:29:11,333 --> 00:29:13,752
Because I wanted something expensive.

424
00:29:15,087 --> 00:29:16,839
Perhaps I shouldn't have said anything.

425
00:29:18,299 --> 00:29:20,050
I shouldn't have asked for anything.

426
00:29:24,096 --> 00:29:25,139
If so,

427
00:29:26,724 --> 00:29:28,017
maybe she would've come back.

428
00:29:56,086 --> 00:29:59,757
They all have
such different aromas and tastes.

429
00:29:59,840 --> 00:30:01,342
I just don't get

430
00:30:01,425 --> 00:30:05,054
why supermarkets or convenience stores
don't sell these delicious liquors.

431
00:30:06,013 --> 00:30:08,140
They have all kinds of soju and beer.

432
00:30:09,141 --> 00:30:10,351
Right?

433
00:30:10,434 --> 00:30:14,647
They'd be much more approachable
if only more retailers picked them up.

434
00:30:15,481 --> 00:30:16,941
As you can see for yourself,

435
00:30:17,024 --> 00:30:19,818
they're not too expensive either.

436
00:30:19,902 --> 00:30:23,572
Of course,
there are some fancy limited editions.

437
00:30:52,476 --> 00:30:53,769
"Jehyangju."

438
00:30:53,852 --> 00:30:55,854
That bottle is empty.

439
00:30:57,898 --> 00:31:01,735
We kept it as a souvenir afterwards.

440
00:31:06,365 --> 00:31:09,034
Was this wine discontinued?

441
00:31:09,618 --> 00:31:10,494
Yes.

442
00:31:10,578 --> 00:31:12,288
They closed the whole brewery.

443
00:31:12,371 --> 00:31:13,747
It was too much work for them.

444
00:31:13,831 --> 00:31:16,542
We barely managed to get that one bottle.

445
00:31:16,625 --> 00:31:19,545
We drank the last drop two days ago.

446
00:31:20,379 --> 00:31:21,755
That empty bottle is all we have.

447
00:31:23,549 --> 00:31:25,593
It was a really fine home-brewed liquor

448
00:31:25,676 --> 00:31:29,763
produced by the Hamyang Park clan
since the Unified Silla era.

449
00:31:31,307 --> 00:31:35,185
It's a great shame,
but it happens a lot in this industry.

450
00:31:35,853 --> 00:31:37,062
I see.

451
00:31:43,319 --> 00:31:44,361
JEHYANGJU

452
00:32:35,037 --> 00:32:37,748
What is this fresh blue-green smell?

453
00:32:49,677 --> 00:32:50,719
Lotus flowers.

454
00:33:35,139 --> 00:33:36,432
The smell of lotus blossoms.

455
00:33:37,975 --> 00:33:39,685
JEHYANGJU

456
00:33:44,898 --> 00:33:47,693
On the left is a distilled liquor
we'll release next month.

457
00:33:47,776 --> 00:33:51,405
On the right is a yakju
we'll release a week later.

458
00:33:51,488 --> 00:33:55,409
With the distilled liquor, we tried to
shed the conventional image

459
00:33:55,492 --> 00:33:58,454
by giving it a clean, fresh taste
the younger people prefer.

460
00:33:59,204 --> 00:34:00,956
It's 28% alcohol.

461
00:34:01,039 --> 00:34:02,166
On the right is a yakju

462
00:34:02,249 --> 00:34:05,085
where ginger and bellflower root
are added in the fermenting stage.

463
00:34:05,169 --> 00:34:10,257
We at Pyeongyeon F&B
have high hopes for this yakju.

464
00:34:10,340 --> 00:34:12,718
According to a poll
we conducted ourselves,

465
00:34:12,801 --> 00:34:15,763
people aged between 20 and 49 bought

466
00:34:15,846 --> 00:34:18,557
much less wine and beer in the last year.

467
00:34:18,640 --> 00:34:21,727
But soju and whiskey sales
remained the same.

468
00:34:21,810 --> 00:34:24,521
That means consumers are turning away

469
00:34:24,605 --> 00:34:28,275
from wine and beer
to other low-alcohol options.

470
00:34:29,359 --> 00:34:31,320
That's why we came up

471
00:34:31,403 --> 00:34:33,906
with the new traditional liquor
with 15% alcohol content.

472
00:34:33,989 --> 00:34:38,577
I heard the next mission
on The Table: K-Chef 2026

473
00:34:38,660 --> 00:34:40,496
involves Korean traditional liquor.

474
00:34:42,414 --> 00:34:46,293
If this round could center
on one of our new releases,

475
00:34:46,376 --> 00:34:47,628
that would be great for PR.

476
00:34:50,005 --> 00:34:53,425
Assuming Ms. Seon is willing
to discuss the matter with us, that is.

477
00:34:54,468 --> 00:34:55,844
What if she did?

478
00:34:56,595 --> 00:34:59,681
People will say we used the show
to promote our products.

479
00:35:00,265 --> 00:35:02,142
Would that benefit our brand image?

480
00:35:02,726 --> 00:35:03,852
Times have changed.

481
00:35:03,936 --> 00:35:05,813
The consumers are way ahead of us!

482
00:35:06,396 --> 00:35:09,566
-I apologize, ma'am.
-I agree wholeheartedly.

483
00:35:11,318 --> 00:35:14,696
I'm sorry, but there's no time
to discuss anything with you.

484
00:35:15,781 --> 00:35:17,991
The third round of the main competition

485
00:35:18,992 --> 00:35:20,327
will air live this Sunday.

486
00:35:21,286 --> 00:35:22,204
What?

487
00:35:23,997 --> 00:35:24,832
It'll air live?

488
00:35:26,625 --> 00:35:29,253
What is this lukewarm response?

489
00:35:29,837 --> 00:35:31,129
Did I startle you that much?

490
00:35:31,213 --> 00:35:32,673
Don't you worry.

491
00:35:32,756 --> 00:35:35,717
We shuffled the schedule
and negotiated with our sponsors.

492
00:35:35,801 --> 00:35:36,635
What do you mean?

493
00:35:37,386 --> 00:35:39,263
Please, think about it.

494
00:35:39,346 --> 00:35:42,182
Everyone's talking about our show.

495
00:35:42,266 --> 00:35:44,309
Ratings are going up with each episode,

496
00:35:44,393 --> 00:35:46,186
and it's the biggest
topic of conversation.

497
00:35:46,270 --> 00:35:49,064
It's our most successful show ever.
What should we do about it?

498
00:35:49,147 --> 00:35:51,316
We thank the viewers
with an even better show.

499
00:35:51,400 --> 00:35:52,526
No scripts.

500
00:35:52,609 --> 00:35:54,736
Completely unedited and raw!

501
00:35:54,820 --> 00:35:56,488
We show the chefs in action, all live.

502
00:35:57,781 --> 00:36:00,450
Amazing! So fantastic!

503
00:36:00,534 --> 00:36:01,660
Think about it.

504
00:36:03,328 --> 00:36:07,207
This is what the viewers want.

505
00:36:07,291 --> 00:36:09,793
A reality show that feels
more real than reality itself.

506
00:36:10,627 --> 00:36:13,422
Why can't we at Pyeongyeon Group

507
00:36:13,505 --> 00:36:17,718
do what ordinary people
do every day on social media?

508
00:36:20,220 --> 00:36:21,471
Don't you agree, Chairwoman?

509
00:36:25,642 --> 00:36:26,602
Go on.

510
00:36:26,685 --> 00:36:28,061
Tell me what you're up to.

511
00:36:30,731 --> 00:36:32,190
I'm not up to anything.

512
00:36:32,774 --> 00:36:33,609
Hae-seon.

513
00:36:35,444 --> 00:36:37,654
What people care about

514
00:36:38,322 --> 00:36:39,448
are stories.

515
00:36:39,531 --> 00:36:43,452
Let's not use missions or strategy
to drag things out.

516
00:36:43,535 --> 00:36:47,164
Let's show the public
what the remaining chefs are really like.

517
00:36:47,247 --> 00:36:48,624
Who knows?

518
00:36:48,707 --> 00:36:52,294
One of them could have
a really interesting story.

519
00:36:53,795 --> 00:36:55,005
Personally,

520
00:36:55,505 --> 00:36:59,760
I want to learn more
about the two adoptees from the US.

521
00:36:59,843 --> 00:37:00,677
Especially him.

522
00:37:01,511 --> 00:37:02,512
Chris K.

523
00:37:04,681 --> 00:37:06,558
I guess I'm Korean through and through.

524
00:37:07,267 --> 00:37:10,187
I find myself wondering
if there is a secret family relationship.

525
00:37:10,771 --> 00:37:11,688
On top of that…

526
00:37:15,108 --> 00:37:16,443
He's handsome too.

527
00:37:18,820 --> 00:37:20,072
He's just my type.

528
00:37:21,281 --> 00:37:22,699
I've explained so many times.

529
00:37:23,992 --> 00:37:25,869
This doesn't taste like
authentic Korean food.

530
00:37:26,453 --> 00:37:28,956
It's just something kids would love.

531
00:37:29,039 --> 00:37:32,542
Our mission is to elevate Korean cuisine.

532
00:37:32,626 --> 00:37:35,295
A half-baked fusion dish
won't stand a chance.

533
00:37:36,838 --> 00:37:38,006
Chef.

534
00:37:38,674 --> 00:37:41,301
Our task is to refine something
we enjoyed as school children.

535
00:37:41,385 --> 00:37:43,971
So I don't think
tteokbokki is a bad choice.

536
00:37:44,554 --> 00:37:48,809
Why don't we make
soy-sauce-based royal tteokbokki?

537
00:37:52,354 --> 00:37:53,605
That's not enough.

538
00:37:54,189 --> 00:37:58,110
How about adding various toppings
to serve it like a fancy sinseonro?

539
00:38:06,451 --> 00:38:09,329
SINSEONRO

540
00:38:20,424 --> 00:38:22,592
That would look impressive and luxurious,

541
00:38:23,552 --> 00:38:26,346
but it won't evoke memories of street food
from people's childhood.

542
00:38:26,430 --> 00:38:28,557
Authenticity is important,

543
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:33,437
but shouldn't we think of the judges
who will actually taste our food?

544
00:38:35,397 --> 00:38:38,066
I think we should cook
something more accessible

545
00:38:38,567 --> 00:38:41,278
to remind them of their happy childhoods.

546
00:38:47,451 --> 00:38:48,326
You two.

547
00:38:50,162 --> 00:38:51,830
You don't know Korean cuisine.

548
00:38:53,457 --> 00:38:55,709
You just cook what looks Korean.

549
00:38:55,792 --> 00:38:58,045
You mix gochujang,
doenjang, and heavy cream,

550
00:38:58,128 --> 00:38:59,755
and season with soy sauce
or salted shrimp.

551
00:38:59,838 --> 00:39:02,090
Do you think
all that looks Korean is Korean?

552
00:39:02,841 --> 00:39:05,594
Have you ever studied the soul
and history behind the food?

553
00:39:08,680 --> 00:39:10,140
What do you think is Korean cuisine?

554
00:39:11,933 --> 00:39:13,977
How much time have you spent
thinking about it?

555
00:39:23,028 --> 00:39:24,071
If that's how you feel…

556
00:39:26,698 --> 00:39:30,285
is your royal court cuisine
the only real K-food?

557
00:39:31,036 --> 00:39:31,870
What?

558
00:39:31,953 --> 00:39:34,664
What would you call what we cook
with our heart and soul?

559
00:39:36,541 --> 00:39:37,876
Is it an American burger?

560
00:39:37,959 --> 00:39:40,378
-Is it a Mexican taco?
-Isabel.

561
00:39:40,462 --> 00:39:44,007
Is Eric Clapton's music not blues
because he's British?

562
00:39:44,091 --> 00:39:46,218
Because he's not a Black man
from the American South?

563
00:39:46,301 --> 00:39:47,928
What about BTS's songs?

564
00:39:48,011 --> 00:39:50,931
Are they American dance pop
because they're not traditional Korean?

565
00:39:51,431 --> 00:39:52,265
Is that not K-pop?

566
00:39:52,349 --> 00:39:53,892
-Isabel, enough.
-People like us…

567
00:39:56,520 --> 00:40:00,649
We also tirelessly study and examine
what true K-food is.

568
00:40:00,732 --> 00:40:04,402
Our restaurants serve
the end result of our studies.

569
00:40:04,486 --> 00:40:07,322
If that isn't K-food, what is?

570
00:40:10,826 --> 00:40:11,993
I guess I wouldn't know.

571
00:40:35,016 --> 00:40:36,226
The kitchens

572
00:40:37,102 --> 00:40:38,145
all look the same.

573
00:40:40,313 --> 00:40:41,231
This isn't mine.

574
00:41:01,918 --> 00:41:05,881
She just had to jump out
at that instant, huh?

575
00:41:06,882 --> 00:41:09,467
Seriously, that old woman…

576
00:41:14,055 --> 00:41:15,682
It's nothing at all.

577
00:41:17,225 --> 00:41:19,895
So, what are you making over here?

578
00:41:19,978 --> 00:41:22,731
We're working on a mixture
of pheasant and seafood broth.

579
00:41:22,814 --> 00:41:24,691
-Pheasant?
-It tastes amazing.

580
00:41:24,774 --> 00:41:27,235
It would make an awesome broth
for fishcake soup.

581
00:41:28,069 --> 00:41:28,987
You think so?

582
00:41:40,457 --> 00:41:41,416
Not bad.

583
00:41:44,252 --> 00:41:46,838
By the way, have you gotten anywhere?

584
00:41:47,339 --> 00:41:48,924
With The Tale of Hong Gil-dong lead.

585
00:41:55,430 --> 00:41:56,932
This is maddening.

586
00:41:57,766 --> 00:42:00,101
What liquor will they ask us to use?

587
00:42:00,185 --> 00:42:04,439
We need at least a rough idea
if we're going to cook anything.

588
00:42:20,997 --> 00:42:22,165
It's frustrating, isn't it?

589
00:42:26,127 --> 00:42:27,796
Don't take it personally.

590
00:42:28,922 --> 00:42:31,424
They have a point too.

591
00:42:36,972 --> 00:42:40,684
I think the crew asked us
to work with street food we loved as kids

592
00:42:42,394 --> 00:42:46,731
so that we would come up with something
less complicated and more accessible.

593
00:42:51,361 --> 00:42:52,445
Let's go back inside.

594
00:42:54,948 --> 00:42:58,785
Let's go inside
and put our heads together, Mother.

595
00:43:08,420 --> 00:43:10,380
No. They keep clashing for some reason.

596
00:43:12,257 --> 00:43:14,134
Any yakju that contains medicinal herbs

597
00:43:14,217 --> 00:43:16,845
should be paired with a dish
with a powerful kick.

598
00:43:20,307 --> 00:43:22,392
Each liquor has its distinctive character.

599
00:43:22,475 --> 00:43:26,855
So the liquor we're given to work with
will determine which direction we take.

600
00:43:26,938 --> 00:43:27,897
-Right?
-Yes.

601
00:43:27,981 --> 00:43:29,566
Who here doesn't know that?

602
00:43:29,649 --> 00:43:30,734
Oh, sorry.

603
00:43:31,776 --> 00:43:33,695
The liquors we tried so far

604
00:43:33,778 --> 00:43:36,656
have a strong aroma
of a certain herb or ingredient.

605
00:43:36,740 --> 00:43:41,619
I get the feeling that the alcohol
is overpowering the food.

606
00:43:45,665 --> 00:43:47,292
You don't like what I brought back?

607
00:43:48,835 --> 00:43:50,378
That's not what I meant.

608
00:43:52,464 --> 00:43:55,300
Minus is bigger than plus.

609
00:43:55,383 --> 00:43:57,594
Bigger? More of?

610
00:43:57,677 --> 00:43:59,304
You subtract

611
00:43:59,846 --> 00:44:01,556
more than you add?

612
00:44:01,639 --> 00:44:03,683
Take out rather than put in?

613
00:44:05,310 --> 00:44:06,311
Here.

614
00:44:09,147 --> 00:44:12,359
I wrapped the sundae stuffing
in a buckwheat crepe, not pig intestines,

615
00:44:12,442 --> 00:44:14,986
coated it in glutinous rice powder,
then deep-fried it.

616
00:44:15,695 --> 00:44:18,948
Underneath are radish sprouts
stir-fried in cold-pressed sesame oil.

617
00:44:28,124 --> 00:44:29,417
-Gosh.
-Goodness.

618
00:44:30,210 --> 00:44:31,044
What's the sauce?

619
00:44:31,795 --> 00:44:33,671
I soaked and ground
newly harvested rice to make roux,

620
00:44:33,755 --> 00:44:35,340
reduced it
with Korean black raspberry wine,

621
00:44:35,423 --> 00:44:37,425
and then added gelatin to thicken it.

622
00:44:41,471 --> 00:44:43,723
He made a thick sauce
rather than a thin one

623
00:44:43,807 --> 00:44:45,975
to keep the fried food from getting soggy.

624
00:44:46,059 --> 00:44:48,978
Where's this sweetness from?
It's not honey or sugar.

625
00:44:53,858 --> 00:44:55,235
Dried persimmons from Sangju.

626
00:45:05,495 --> 00:45:07,038
It's so nice.

627
00:45:07,122 --> 00:45:10,041
-It's a taste from our childhood.
-Right.

628
00:45:11,126 --> 00:45:12,085
Pretty good.

629
00:45:12,877 --> 00:45:13,753
It's a great idea.

630
00:45:14,504 --> 00:45:17,048
The sweetness
of the dried persimmon is familiar,

631
00:45:17,132 --> 00:45:18,800
but it also adds a twist to the flavor.

632
00:45:19,634 --> 00:45:20,468
Is that why?

633
00:45:20,552 --> 00:45:23,096
It feels like home-cooked food.
A fancy one at that.

634
00:45:40,822 --> 00:45:44,492
It seems they both like
what you prepared, Mother.

635
00:45:45,160 --> 00:45:47,162
They say it's like
a mom's home-cooked food.

636
00:45:53,877 --> 00:45:55,128
Something that feels like home.

637
00:45:56,337 --> 00:45:57,213
A home-cooked meal

638
00:45:58,465 --> 00:45:59,632
and a drink to go with it.

639
00:46:00,550 --> 00:46:01,843
Good.

640
00:46:02,427 --> 00:46:03,261
On top of that,

641
00:46:05,221 --> 00:46:07,557
we'll hit them with a scent from the past.

642
00:46:07,640 --> 00:46:10,435
Let's take this round with aroma.

643
00:46:12,145 --> 00:46:13,521
SCORCHED RICE

644
00:46:13,605 --> 00:46:14,814
CORN TEA

645
00:46:22,197 --> 00:46:23,448
We'll go live?

646
00:46:23,531 --> 00:46:26,284
Are you saying
this round will be broadcast live?

647
00:46:28,244 --> 00:46:29,329
Yes, that's right.

648
00:46:30,205 --> 00:46:31,039
We'll go live?

649
00:46:31,122 --> 00:46:32,248
For real?

650
00:46:32,332 --> 00:46:34,083
A live broadcast means…

651
00:46:34,167 --> 00:46:37,212
Everything we do will air in real time.

652
00:46:37,754 --> 00:46:39,797
Including every single tiny mistake.

653
00:46:39,881 --> 00:46:40,965
Jesus.

654
00:46:42,800 --> 00:46:46,930
This Sunday's broadcast
will be split into two parts.

655
00:46:47,013 --> 00:46:49,766
To be more specific,
the actual competition in the second part

656
00:46:49,849 --> 00:46:51,100
will be aired live.

657
00:46:51,184 --> 00:46:54,771
The first part will be an edited version
of the teams prepping for the round

658
00:46:54,854 --> 00:46:56,189
and will air at the usual time.

659
00:46:56,272 --> 00:47:00,068
We scheduled the second part
as a special two-hour broadcast.

660
00:47:00,151 --> 00:47:02,195
It'll cover
the actual cooking competition.

661
00:47:03,571 --> 00:47:04,489
No way.

662
00:47:04,572 --> 00:47:05,532
Why are you doing this?

663
00:47:06,574 --> 00:47:08,326
Why did you suddenly schedule

664
00:47:08,910 --> 00:47:10,745
a special live broadcast?

665
00:47:15,500 --> 00:47:19,504
I'd like you to take it as a sign
of the network's enthusiasm.

666
00:47:19,587 --> 00:47:21,756
At the moment,
everyone is talking about the show.

667
00:47:21,839 --> 00:47:24,676
We want to surprise the viewers
with something they would never expect

668
00:47:24,759 --> 00:47:28,012
to make you and your dishes
stand out even more.

669
00:47:29,389 --> 00:47:33,059
Since we're putting you
under additional pressure,

670
00:47:33,142 --> 00:47:36,271
we're giving you a special benefit.

671
00:47:37,105 --> 00:47:40,775
Each team received a hint in the van.

672
00:47:40,858 --> 00:47:42,569
You can discuss them with the other teams.

673
00:47:43,152 --> 00:47:44,070
As for those hints…

674
00:47:44,153 --> 00:47:45,863
CANNOT ADDRESS HIS FATHER AS "FATHER"

675
00:47:45,947 --> 00:47:46,990
Minus?

676
00:47:51,869 --> 00:47:53,580
They're about the liquor

677
00:47:54,205 --> 00:47:55,915
that will appear as part of the mission.

678
00:48:00,712 --> 00:48:03,590
I chose the worst day to come by.

679
00:48:07,302 --> 00:48:10,013
The price rises after you sell
and drops after you buy.

680
00:48:10,096 --> 00:48:14,100
Such is the fate of a man
who can't see into the future.

681
00:48:20,231 --> 00:48:21,399
Just a minute.

682
00:48:27,947 --> 00:48:31,409
What's with this bad energy?

683
00:48:31,492 --> 00:48:33,536
There are evil spirits everywhere.

684
00:48:35,538 --> 00:48:36,372
Hey!

685
00:48:37,081 --> 00:48:38,166
Out!

686
00:48:38,249 --> 00:48:39,709
Get out of here!

687
00:48:40,668 --> 00:48:43,254
Don't they pray
to their house guardian or kitchen god?

688
00:48:43,338 --> 00:48:45,548
They need to take
better care of their land.

689
00:48:46,549 --> 00:48:48,801
Someone could get badly hurt.

690
00:49:00,938 --> 00:49:02,523
You know this city, right?

691
00:49:02,607 --> 00:49:03,775
"Naples."

692
00:49:03,858 --> 00:49:04,901
-Naples.
-Naples.

693
00:49:04,984 --> 00:49:06,235
Naples.

694
00:49:06,319 --> 00:49:07,320
-Naples.
-Naples.

695
00:49:07,403 --> 00:49:10,239
Very good. This is a city in Australia.

696
00:49:10,823 --> 00:49:11,824
"Melbourne."

697
00:49:11,908 --> 00:49:12,992
-Melbourne.
-Melbourne.

698
00:49:13,076 --> 00:49:14,285
Melbourne.

699
00:49:14,369 --> 00:49:15,411
-Melbourne.
-Melbourne.

700
00:49:15,495 --> 00:49:17,622
The O and U connect smoothly.

701
00:49:17,705 --> 00:49:18,539
Melbourne.

702
00:49:18,623 --> 00:49:20,083
-Melbourne.
-Melbourne.

703
00:49:20,166 --> 00:49:22,085
Good. One more time. Melbourne.

704
00:49:22,168 --> 00:49:24,045
-Melbourne.
-Good.

705
00:49:26,422 --> 00:49:27,256
"Berlin."

706
00:49:29,509 --> 00:49:30,677
Berlin.

707
00:49:31,260 --> 00:49:32,303
But that sounds wrong.

708
00:49:32,387 --> 00:49:34,514
No, it's different
from the Korean pronunciation.

709
00:49:34,597 --> 00:49:35,431
It's Berlin.

710
00:49:36,349 --> 00:49:37,558
-Berlin.
-Berlin.

711
00:49:37,642 --> 00:49:39,394
One more time. Berlin.

712
00:49:39,477 --> 00:49:41,062
-Berlin.
-Berlin.

713
00:49:41,145 --> 00:49:42,146
Perfect.

714
00:49:42,230 --> 00:49:43,439
I used to live here.

715
00:49:44,148 --> 00:49:44,982
"Manhattan."

716
00:49:48,778 --> 00:49:49,612
Manhattan.

717
00:49:49,696 --> 00:49:51,572
Manhattan.

718
00:49:54,992 --> 00:49:57,161
I guess this one is a bit tough.

719
00:49:57,245 --> 00:49:59,914
The accent is on the second syllable.

720
00:49:59,997 --> 00:50:00,832
Manhattan.

721
00:50:01,457 --> 00:50:02,792
Manhattan.

722
00:50:03,751 --> 00:50:05,086
Don't pronounce the T.

723
00:50:05,169 --> 00:50:06,003
Manhattan.

724
00:50:06,087 --> 00:50:07,338
Manhattan.

725
00:50:07,422 --> 00:50:08,673
Yes. Once more.

726
00:50:08,756 --> 00:50:09,674
Manhattan.

727
00:50:09,757 --> 00:50:11,050
-Manhattan.
-Manhattan.

728
00:50:11,134 --> 00:50:12,510
Have some confidence. Manhattan.

729
00:50:12,593 --> 00:50:13,553
-Manhattan.
-Manhattan.

730
00:50:13,636 --> 00:50:15,430
Yes. Good job, guys. Perfect. High-five.

731
00:50:16,139 --> 00:50:17,181
Up here.

732
00:50:25,523 --> 00:50:26,691
Manhattan.

733
00:50:28,067 --> 00:50:30,069
Let's go back to the top.

734
00:50:30,153 --> 00:50:34,490
A SPECIAL COOKING COMPETITION AT HAEON
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026

735
00:50:37,201 --> 00:50:38,286
Thank you so much.

736
00:50:38,995 --> 00:50:42,790
Our students almost never get to
hear a native speaker speak.

737
00:50:43,750 --> 00:50:45,126
My pronunciation isn't great either.

738
00:50:45,209 --> 00:50:46,169
It was no trouble.

739
00:50:46,753 --> 00:50:49,255
I'm pleased I could be of some help.

740
00:50:49,338 --> 00:50:50,840
You must be so busy.

741
00:50:50,923 --> 00:50:53,468
Thank you again for sparing your time.

742
00:50:53,551 --> 00:50:55,303
-I have to get back inside.
-Sure.

743
00:50:55,386 --> 00:50:56,304
Goodbye then.

744
00:51:03,144 --> 00:51:05,772
When did you volunteer
as a teacher for the day?

745
00:51:05,855 --> 00:51:07,565
You're so unpredictable.

746
00:51:07,648 --> 00:51:09,609
I came to interview the teachers,

747
00:51:10,318 --> 00:51:11,778
and she suddenly asked for my help.

748
00:51:11,861 --> 00:51:14,238
Interview the teachers? For what?

749
00:51:14,989 --> 00:51:17,200
The judges for this round
are graduates of this school.

750
00:51:18,326 --> 00:51:19,911
I thought it would help to know

751
00:51:19,994 --> 00:51:22,121
what they liked
and what sells well nearby.

752
00:51:22,747 --> 00:51:23,998
So I came by.

753
00:51:24,957 --> 00:51:25,833
Grandpa!

754
00:51:29,962 --> 00:51:31,464
Come here, quick.

755
00:51:32,048 --> 00:51:35,843
Grandpa. He taught us English today.

756
00:51:35,927 --> 00:51:38,304
His pronunciation is awesome.

757
00:51:38,888 --> 00:51:40,348
It's straight from an American drama.

758
00:51:40,848 --> 00:51:42,809
That's right. You're so cool.

759
00:51:42,892 --> 00:51:43,768
Am I?

760
00:51:45,394 --> 00:51:48,481
You're one of the chefs
from Seoul, aren't you?

761
00:51:49,524 --> 00:51:51,108
Yes, that's right, sir.

762
00:51:52,944 --> 00:51:53,903
A chef?

763
00:51:55,530 --> 00:51:58,199
Yes, kids. I'm a chef.

764
00:51:58,282 --> 00:51:59,826
Hey, Chef.

765
00:52:01,285 --> 00:52:02,537
Let's go home.

766
00:52:02,620 --> 00:52:04,664
After you wash up,
we can have sliced watermelon.

767
00:52:04,747 --> 00:52:07,083
I bought a watermelon and some ice cream.

768
00:52:07,166 --> 00:52:08,501
Awesome!

769
00:52:08,584 --> 00:52:09,794
Let's hurry, Grandpa.

770
00:52:09,877 --> 00:52:11,003
Will you join us?

771
00:52:11,087 --> 00:52:12,213
I'd love to.

772
00:52:16,342 --> 00:52:17,301
Bye.

773
00:52:18,761 --> 00:52:19,887
We should get back too.

774
00:52:22,515 --> 00:52:23,349
Song?

775
00:52:25,810 --> 00:52:26,686
What is it?

776
00:52:28,062 --> 00:52:29,105
Lotus flowers.

777
00:52:29,188 --> 00:52:30,189
What?

778
00:52:31,357 --> 00:52:33,776
That old gentleman
smelled of lotus flowers.

779
00:52:46,914 --> 00:52:48,374
JEHYANGJU

780
00:52:48,457 --> 00:52:49,458
LOTUS-FLOWER-SCENTED JEHYANGJU

781
00:52:51,127 --> 00:52:52,795
MADE WITH RICE FROM HAEON
AND TRADITIONAL YEAST

782
00:52:54,463 --> 00:52:57,425
WHAT TO DRINK WHEN AT HAEON:
A TRADITIONAL LIQUOR FAN TRIES JEHYANGJU

783
00:52:58,968 --> 00:53:01,429
A WATERY YET FLOWERY SCENT,
A DRINK LIKE THE SCENERY…

784
00:53:03,514 --> 00:53:07,768
JEHYANGJU BREWER PARK SEON-WOONG
KEEPS THE TRADITION ALIVE

785
00:53:10,187 --> 00:53:12,690
RICE STEAMED WITH MEDICINAL HERBS

786
00:53:26,037 --> 00:53:27,455
I told you this morning.

787
00:53:27,538 --> 00:53:29,832
It needs to sit for half a day.

788
00:53:30,416 --> 00:53:32,335
At least half a day, I said!

789
00:53:32,418 --> 00:53:35,004
What am I to do with a hardened dough?

790
00:53:36,422 --> 00:53:38,424
I apologize, Chef. I'm sorry.

791
00:53:38,507 --> 00:53:39,967
I was getting other ingredients and…

792
00:53:40,593 --> 00:53:42,094
I forgot about the dough.

793
00:53:42,178 --> 00:53:44,513
Of all things to forget about!
That's a rookie mistake!

794
00:53:46,390 --> 00:53:48,225
What got into your head?

795
00:53:48,309 --> 00:53:50,895
You seem to think we're on holiday.

796
00:53:52,313 --> 00:53:54,482
Do you know why
I chose you and not Michael?

797
00:53:55,483 --> 00:53:57,443
If you can't even do this properly,

798
00:53:58,486 --> 00:53:59,654
pack up and go back to Seoul.

799
00:53:59,737 --> 00:54:02,573
I have no use
for a pathetic sous-chef like you.

800
00:54:15,169 --> 00:54:16,003
Chef.

801
00:54:17,713 --> 00:54:18,881
I think…

802
00:54:21,133 --> 00:54:22,677
I think I might…

803
00:54:23,594 --> 00:54:24,720
What?

804
00:54:25,972 --> 00:54:27,056
I'm sorry, Chef.

805
00:54:29,225 --> 00:54:30,142
I keep

806
00:54:31,644 --> 00:54:32,895
losing confidence.

807
00:54:34,021 --> 00:54:34,939
I'm afraid

808
00:54:35,898 --> 00:54:37,733
I'll ruin the competition for you.

809
00:54:40,611 --> 00:54:41,570
You should've

810
00:54:43,072 --> 00:54:44,991
chosen someone other than me.

811
00:54:45,074 --> 00:54:46,075
I'm sorry, Chef.

812
00:54:51,747 --> 00:54:52,707
Is that it?

813
00:54:55,543 --> 00:54:56,419
You…

814
00:54:59,839 --> 00:55:00,923
Is this all you can do?

815
00:55:07,221 --> 00:55:08,597
Get out of my sight.

816
00:55:10,933 --> 00:55:12,226
Right now!

817
00:55:45,509 --> 00:55:46,510
Hey.

818
00:55:55,186 --> 00:55:57,646
Did you have to go that far
over one mistake?

819
00:56:00,858 --> 00:56:03,319
We're a team. We're in this together.

820
00:56:04,111 --> 00:56:05,071
We should help each other--

821
00:56:05,154 --> 00:56:07,656
My team can't afford such mistakes.

822
00:56:07,740 --> 00:56:08,699
My team…

823
00:56:09,658 --> 00:56:11,327
doesn't make stupid mistakes.

824
00:56:12,203 --> 00:56:14,038
This isn't your team.

825
00:56:14,997 --> 00:56:15,915
It's our team.

826
00:56:18,667 --> 00:56:19,752
"Our team"?

827
00:56:21,462 --> 00:56:23,964
Listen, Chris K.

828
00:56:27,259 --> 00:56:28,219
You might think

829
00:56:30,054 --> 00:56:32,431
this is some school talent show.

830
00:56:32,515 --> 00:56:33,557
But to me…

831
00:56:36,268 --> 00:56:38,145
it's something I have to give my all to.

832
00:56:40,439 --> 00:56:41,649
It's the reason I exist.

833
00:56:45,069 --> 00:56:48,989
Every single moment,
every single dish I make!

834
00:56:52,493 --> 00:56:54,954
Why is it like that for you?

835
00:56:57,331 --> 00:56:59,875
It's just one delicious meal.

836
00:57:02,670 --> 00:57:04,672
It's not about life or death.

837
00:57:07,842 --> 00:57:08,676
What?

838
00:57:11,679 --> 00:57:12,805
Tae-seop.

839
00:57:15,641 --> 00:57:18,060
He didn't deserve to die
for what he did that day.

840
00:57:21,981 --> 00:57:22,940
Perhaps…

841
00:57:25,734 --> 00:57:28,070
Perhaps Tae-seop did nothing wrong.

842
00:57:28,654 --> 00:57:29,572
You little…

843
00:57:30,531 --> 00:57:31,532
What did you say?

844
00:57:33,159 --> 00:57:34,785
What did you just say?

845
00:57:35,494 --> 00:57:37,037
I'd like to ask the questions.

846
00:57:38,539 --> 00:57:40,875
Was it really Tae-seop
who messed up that day?

847
00:57:41,542 --> 00:57:44,962
Or did he take the fall for someone else?

848
00:57:49,592 --> 00:57:51,719
The fridge was set at 10°C, not 3°C?

849
00:57:53,929 --> 00:57:54,847
Do you know…

850
00:57:55,973 --> 00:57:57,266
what it took to get that ingredient?

851
00:57:59,685 --> 00:58:01,729
Do you know how much work it took?

852
00:58:33,469 --> 00:58:34,803
No one else might know,

853
00:58:38,224 --> 00:58:39,642
but the restaurant's head chef…

854
00:58:42,645 --> 00:58:43,771
would know something.

855
00:59:28,315 --> 00:59:29,191
Is this the place?

856
00:59:38,826 --> 00:59:39,660
Hello?

857
00:59:41,745 --> 00:59:42,871
Is anyone home?

858
00:59:46,000 --> 00:59:49,962
Song. It's too late.
Let's come back tomorrow morning.

859
00:59:55,092 --> 00:59:56,552
Is anyone in?

860
01:00:10,024 --> 01:00:11,191
Song.

861
01:00:16,030 --> 01:00:17,489
Darn it.

862
01:00:40,429 --> 01:00:42,139
Excuse me, sir.

863
01:00:44,975 --> 01:00:46,185
Who are you?

864
01:00:46,268 --> 01:00:49,063
We met briefly earlier today.

865
01:01:07,122 --> 01:01:09,958
Those are the last two crates' worth
from the last batch I made.

866
01:01:11,210 --> 01:01:13,128
A crate and a half, to be exact.

867
01:01:15,547 --> 01:01:18,384
By the way,
what brought you all the way here?

868
01:01:21,261 --> 01:01:24,098
I don't make cheongju anymore.

869
01:01:25,974 --> 01:01:27,226
Why not?

870
01:01:27,309 --> 01:01:29,812
Why did you stop?

871
01:01:39,029 --> 01:01:40,948
JEHYANGJU

872
01:01:41,031 --> 01:01:43,784
Would you like a taste?

873
01:01:47,287 --> 01:01:49,206
Yes, please.

874
01:02:00,384 --> 01:02:01,593
Thank you.

875
01:02:35,419 --> 01:02:36,378
Lotus flowers.

876
01:02:40,674 --> 01:02:42,217
I smell lotus flowers.

877
01:02:42,926 --> 01:02:46,597
The subtle scent
I noticed from the empty bottle.

878
01:02:47,389 --> 01:02:50,517
I visited a temple as a child with my dad,

879
01:02:50,601 --> 01:02:53,604
and we walked by a pond
covered in lotus flowers.

880
01:02:54,188 --> 01:02:56,690
This is exactly what I smelled that day.

881
01:03:04,072 --> 01:03:05,782
Lotus blossoms.

882
01:03:06,658 --> 01:03:08,202
The smell of my dad.

883
01:03:12,414 --> 01:03:13,499
The scent of this drink

884
01:03:14,500 --> 01:03:16,126
reminds me of my father.

885
01:03:23,884 --> 01:03:25,010
Cheonghyang.

886
01:03:27,971 --> 01:03:29,848
My father mentioned it to me
a long time ago.

887
01:03:31,975 --> 01:03:32,976
Cheonghyang.

888
01:03:35,562 --> 01:03:37,064
You don't smell it with your nose.

889
01:03:40,234 --> 01:03:43,153
You feel it in your head.

890
01:03:46,073 --> 01:03:47,282
The scent of wind and air…

891
01:03:49,576 --> 01:03:50,994
that you feel with your whole body.

892
01:03:56,333 --> 01:03:57,251
This liquor.

893
01:04:00,128 --> 01:04:02,005
It has cheonghyang.

894
01:04:06,760 --> 01:04:08,428
The very aroma…

895
01:04:10,389 --> 01:04:11,431
my father told me about.

896
01:04:14,268 --> 01:04:15,269
Who is that man?

897
01:04:25,654 --> 01:04:26,488
Cheonghyang.

898
01:04:28,031 --> 01:04:30,909
You said your father
told you about cheonghyang.

899
01:04:35,747 --> 01:04:36,915
Who is he?

900
01:05:32,387 --> 01:05:34,014
Do you know each other?

901
01:05:34,097 --> 01:05:35,724
What are you hiding?

902
01:05:38,393 --> 01:05:39,936
My talisman. No!

903
01:05:41,647 --> 01:05:43,065
You can do it.

904
01:05:43,565 --> 01:05:46,610
-You must do it.
-It's the promised time!

905
01:05:46,693 --> 01:05:49,529
I sometimes wonder
which script you're working with.

906
01:05:49,613 --> 01:05:51,490
I really wanted to meet you.

907
01:05:53,408 --> 01:05:54,868
Whatever happens…

908
01:05:54,951 --> 01:05:56,662
Please look at the screen.

909
01:05:56,745 --> 01:05:58,080
What is this?

910
01:05:58,163 --> 01:05:59,581
I believe in you, Chris.

911
01:06:01,166 --> 01:06:02,334
Chris!

912
01:06:06,296 --> 01:06:09,299
Subtitle: Jennifer Jean Lim
